  "summary": "Royal beaded crowns worn by Yoruba kings to represent their sacred connection to orisha deities.",
  "description": "Characterized by vibrant beadwork patterns covering conical forms with faces, veils, and symbolic motifs. These crowns visually manifest the king's intermediary role between human and divine realms, with bead colors and patterns encoding specific spiritual meanings.",
  "culturalContext": "Yoruba beaded crowns (ade) — Yoruba oba's sacred regalia — crown kings in whole-cone beadwork: veils (ibodun) hiding the royal face (the crown contains the ori — head — power), multicolor bead-birds and faces (Yoruba \"live-birds\" proverbs), and full beaded staffs, slippers and panels. The bead tradition (Abeokuta workshops) carries rank-coded color rules; museum collections (British Museum's ijebu crown) and Yoruba contemporary art (Tunde Odunlade) keep the crown alive.",
